.TH ETIQUETTE 1 "2026-08-31" "realisateur" "bashified verb"
.SH NAME
etiquette \- the estate's issue-label grammar, and whether a repository follows it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B etiquette
.br
.B etiquette
--path
.br
.B etiquette
.I owner/repo
.br
.B etiquette
.I owner/repo
--apply
.br
.B etiquette
--all
.RI [ --apply ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Prints the label grammar every repository in this ecosystem follows, and
grades one repository against it.
.PP
The grammar has exactly one home,
.BR bin/lib/labels.tsv ,
and is read at the point of use rather than copied into each repository. A
grammar copied into twenty-four repositories is twenty-four grammars:
stamped copies do not travel, they rot, and the drift detector reports OK
throughout.
.PP
Each row declares a label, its colour, its
.B source
and its meaning. The source column decides who may write the label, and it is
the point of the file:
.TP
.B derived:decision
Line 1 of the issue body declares
.BR DECISION: .
Never typed by hand \(em
.B --apply
writes it, reading
.B bin/lib/body-grammar.sh
for the declaration, and for whether a stated
.B DEFAULT-AFTER
window has lapsed, and editing line 1 is how you change it.
Typed by hand it was wrong three times out of three. This is the same
declaration
.BR gh-sign (1)
enforces when the issue is created, so the label is a view of the body rather
than a second opinion about it.
.PP
An owner who answers OUT LOUD rather than by typing a comment has still
answered \(em but nothing sees it until an agent relays the ruling. Do that
with
.B "<!-- decision-by: <who> -->"
on the comment carrying the ruling, not with the
.B answered
label: that label means the answer lives somewhere this predicate cannot
see at all, and reaching for it when the ruling is right there in the
comment you just wrote spends its audit value for nothing
(hf7y/realisateur#924).
.TP
.BI written: verb
That verb applies the label at filing time and nothing else should. A person
retyping one of these is drift.
.TP
.B typed
A judgement a person or agent applies. No mechanism derives it, and none
should claim to.
.PP
A label absent from the grammar is a repository's own business: it is left
alone and not reported. The grammar declares a floor, not a whitelist.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --path
Print the file the grammar was read from. Use this to prove which copy a host
obeyed.
.TP
.B --apply
Create any declared label the repository lacks, and add or remove the derived
label so it matches each open issue's line 1. Without it, nothing is written.
.PP
.B --apply
never writes a label for an
.B UNDECLARED
body \(em one whose line 1 declares neither. That is a finding about the body,
and the fix is to edit line 1.
.TP
.B --all
Grade every repository named in
.BR bin/lib/roster-set.sh ,
one after another, and report the worst verdict any of them produced. This is
the estate-wide caller, and it is what
.B .github/workflows/etiquette.yml
runs daily with
.BR --apply .
.PP
It sweeps the repository set, not the armed set. The derived label used to be
reconciled only by the per-account dispatch, which grades the repository it is
dispatching to and no other, so a project whose
.B schedule/ROSTER
row reads
.B parked
never acquired the label at all. On 2026-08-31 every one of the eighteen rows
read
.BR parked .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The repository carries every declared label and each derived one matches its
body.
.TP
.B 1
Findings: a declared label is missing, a derived one disagrees with its body,
or a body declares neither.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 6
BLIND \(em the grammar or the issue list could not be read. Never 0: an
unreadable tracker is not a clean one, and a grammar that failed to load is
not an empty grammar.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR defere (1),
.BR gh-sign (1)
